@misc{polavarapu_cross-asset_2026,
	title = {Cross-{Asset} {Market} {Regime} {Detection} {Using} {Hidden} {Markov} {Models}: {A} {Framework} for {Real}-{Time} {Regime}-{Aware} {Risk} {Management}},
	copyright = {https://www.uspto.gov/ip-policy/copyright-policy/copyright-basics},
	shorttitle = {Cross-{Asset} {Market} {Regime} {Detection} {Using} {Hidden} {Markov} {Models}},
	url = {https://www.ssrn.com/abstract=6539358},
	doi = {10.2139/ssrn.6539358},
	abstract = {{\textless}p{\textgreater}Standard quantitative risk models assume financial returns follow a normal distribution with constant volatility. This paper demonstrates empirically that this assumption fails during regime shifts, producing an 11.58 percentage point underestimation of annual tail risk for S\&amp;P 500 returns spanning the 2020 COVID crisis and 2022 Federal Reserve rate shock (January 2020 – December 2024).{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}We implement a cross-asset Gaussian Hidden Markov Model (HMM) — the Kroniq Regime Radar — using nine features across five asset classes: SPY, VIX, TLT, GLD, and ICE BofA US High Yield credit spreads (FRED: BAMLH0A0HYM2). A rigorous BIC model selection sweep across K=3 through K=9, with 5 independent random seeds per K and minimum state-size stability validation, confirms K=5 as the optimal and production-ready state count. Models at K=6, K=7, and K=8 produce unstable micro-states (as few as 9 days), inconsistent with reliable live regime routing.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}The five-state model produces a two-stage early warning for the March 2020 COVID crash: a Macro stress signal on October 1, 2019 — 141 calendar days before the February 19, 2020 SPY price peak — followed by full Crisis classification on February 28, 2020, before 79\% of the total drawdown materialised. This 141-day lead time substantially extended early warning capability relative to the seven-feature baseline (23 days), attributable to the credit spread features; it is event-specific and not assumed to generalise as a consistent predictive horizon. Static out-of-sample performance (2021–2024): Sharpe 1.107, maximum drawdown -8.37\%, CAGR 7.94\%. Walk-forward OOS with quarterly retraining: Sharpe 0.881, MaxDD -17.46\%, CAGR 9.97\% — all exceeding buy-and-hold Sharpe of 0.859.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}
{\textless}p{\textgreater}The five-state transition probability matrix confirms regime persistence (Low-Vol: 95.5\%, Bull: 92.7\%, Neutral: 92.5\%, Macro: 81.7\%, Crisis: 73.0\%) with no direct transitions from calm states to crisis within the observed training sample — consistent with how market dislocations actually unfold. Regime persistence is consistent with macro intuition, with states typically lasting multiple weeks to months rather than exhibiting high-frequency switching. The model demonstrates cross-event generalization across structurally distinct regime types: correctly classifying the fast COVID-2020 dislocation (Crisis state, VIX \&gt; 48) and the prolonged 2022 Federal Reserve rate shock (Macro state, 99.2\% classification purity) without structural modification. All three historical sanity checks pass. The objective is not to predict markets, but to dynamically adapt risk exposure to evolving regimes. These findings motivate the Kroniq framework: a cross-asset HMM regime detection API targeting institutional systematic funds.{\textless}/p{\textgreater}},
	urldate = {2026-08-15},
	author = {Polavarapu, Sumanth},
	year = {2026},
}

